5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The trp attenuator relies on mutually exclusive RNA hairpin structures in the trpL leader transcript. Under tryptophan-replete conditions the ribosome does not stall, covers region 1, and releases at the stop codon so that region 2 is masked when region 3 is transcribed; consequently the only available pairing partner for region 3 is region 4 and the 3-4 terminator forms.  

A mutation that prevents region 1 from base-pairing with region 2 frees region 2 to pair with region 3 as soon as region 3 emerges. Formation of the 2-3 antiterminator hairpin sequesters region 3, blocks 3-4 stem-loop formation, and therefore allows read-through even when tryptophan is high.  

Options B, C, D and E either strengthen the terminator, weaken overall transcription, or have no direct effect on 3-4 pairing, so they cannot produce the described phenotype.}

8.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The trp leader transcript can form alternative RNA hairpins (1-2 pause, 2-3 antiterminator, or 3-4 terminator) whose formation is dictated by ribosome position. Under tryptophan-replete conditions the ribosome translates through region 1 and into region 2, freeing region 3 to pair with region 4 and create the terminator.  

A mutation that abolishes base-pairing between regions 1 and 2 leaves region 2 free to pair with the newly transcribed region 3 as soon as region 3 emerges. Formation of the 2-3 antiterminator sequesters the nucleotides that would otherwise form the 3-4 stem-loop, eliminating termination and allowing read-through even when tryptophan is high.  

The other options either favor terminator formation (B), affect termination efficiency without blocking hairpin formation (C), or are unrelated to hairpin choice (D, E).}

